description:
In handle_metadata_changes (src/metadata.rs:79-102), the same .as_object_mut() pattern fires twice in succession:

    let filter_related_type: Option<String> = args
        .as_object_mut()
        .and_then(|m| m.remove('filterRelatedType'))
        .and_then(...);

    let filter_metadata_type: Option<Vec<String>> = args
        .as_object_mut()
        .and_then(|m| m.remove('filterMetadataType'))
        .and_then(...);

Each call walks the Value enum to confirm it's an Object. Idiomatic Rust either destructures once:

    let (filter_related_type, filter_metadata_type): (Option<String>, Option<Vec<String>>) =
        match args.as_object_mut() {
            Some(m) => {
                let rt = m.remove('filterRelatedType')
                    .and_then(|v| if let Value::String(s) = v { Some(s) } else { None });
                let mt = m.remove('filterMetadataType')
                    .and_then(|v| if let Value::Array(arr) = v {
                        Some(arr.into_iter()
                            .filter_map(|v| if let Value::String(s) = v { Some(s) } else { None })
                            .collect())
                    } else { None });
                (rt, mt)
            }
            None => (None, None),
        };

…or extracts the &mut Map upfront and treats the no-object case explicitly (likely a top-level invalidArguments error, since RFC 8620 §5 requires method args to be a JSON object). Going further: the current code silently treats non-object args as 'no filters present' and proceeds to extract_account_id, which will then fail with its own error — diagnostics quality would improve if the handler rejected non-object args at the top.

What goes wrong: low-priority readability and very small wasted Value-enum-walk cost. The diagnostics-quality wart (silent non-object pass-through) is a separate, more material issue but it's a feature gap, not an idiom miss.

This is P4 (opinion-adjacent). The current shape is acceptable; the suggested shape is incrementally better. Flag for awareness during any future revisit of the function — not for forced rework.
